<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
                   xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                   xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog https://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd"
                   logicalFilePath="config/liquibase/changelog/20260916083344_changelog.xml">

    <!--
    Gives the exercise table the check constraint the application has always assumed: an exercise belongs to a course
    or to an exercise group, never to both and never to neither.

    Exercise#checkCourseAndExerciseGroupExclusivity states the rule, and the create, update and import endpoints of
    every exercise type call it, so requests have been checked for years. The database has not been, and anything
    writing through a repository rather than through a resource is therefore unchecked. A row carrying both is not a
    curiosity: Exercise#isExamExercise tests the exercise group alone, so such a row is an exam exercise whose
    course_id names a course it does not belong to, and every authorization decision derived from that column answers
    about the wrong course.

    Both foreign keys are ON DELETE RESTRICT, so no exercise can be orphaned by deleting its course or its exercise
    group. That bounds what an installation can have accumulated to rows written directly.

    To see what this will change, run before upgrading:

        SELECT id, title,
               CASE WHEN course_id IS NOT NULL AND exercise_group_id IS NOT NULL THEN 'both'
                    ELSE 'neither' END AS violation
        FROM exercise
        WHERE (course_id IS NULL) = (exercise_group_id IS NULL);

    The two violations are not the same problem. An exercise carrying BOTH is repaired, because the repair is not a
    guess: getCourseViaExerciseGroupOrCourseMember already reads such a row through its exam and ignores course_id, so
    clearing course_id writes down what the application believes and changes the answer to no question it asks. An
    exercise carrying NEITHER is neither repaired nor deleted, because nothing in the row says which course it
    belonged to and an exercise owns submissions, results and grades. It halts the upgrade with a message naming it,
    the same choice 20260410144433_changelog.xml makes for a state only a person can resolve.

    Liquibase's addCheckConstraint is a Pro change type, so the DDL is raw SQL; the statement is the same on both
    engines. Enforcing it costs nothing on read: a check constraint is evaluated on INSERT and UPDATE only, and this
    one is two null tests on columns already in the row being written.
    -->

    <!--
    information_schema is portable but the "current schema" function is not, following 20260910212533_changelog.xml.
    Every changeset interpolating ${lbSchema} must carry dbms="mysql,postgresql" so the placeholder does not reach
    another engine verbatim.
    -->
    <property name="lbSchema" value="DATABASE()" dbms="mysql"/>
    <property name="lbSchema" value="current_schema()" dbms="postgresql"/>

    <changeSet id="20260916083344-01-precondition-no-orphaned-exercises" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="HALT" onFailMessage="The exercise table contains rows with neither a course_id nor an exercise_group_id. Such an exercise belongs to nothing, and this migration cannot decide what it belonged to: the row carries no evidence of it, and an exercise owns submissions, results and grades, so it is not deleted either. Find them with: SELECT id, title, discriminator FROM exercise WHERE course_id IS NULL AND exercise_group_id IS NULL; then attach each one to its course or exercise group, or remove it deliberately, and start again.">
            <sqlCheck expectedResult="0">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM exercise WHERE course_id IS NULL AND exercise_group_id IS NULL</sqlCheck>
        </preConditions>
        <!-- No-op body; the precondition is the entire purpose of this changeset. -->
        <sql>SELECT 1</sql>
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260916083344-02-clear-course-on-exam-exercises" author="krusche">
        <comment>Clear course_id on an exercise that also names an exercise group, which the application already reads as an exam exercise.</comment>
        <sql>
            UPDATE exercise SET course_id = NULL WHERE course_id IS NOT NULL AND exercise_group_id IS NOT NULL;
        </sql>
        <!--
        Not recoverable, and not needed. The cleared value named a course the exercise did not belong to, so nothing
        read it: getCourseViaExerciseGroupOrCourseMember has always resolved such a row through its exam.
        -->
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
    MARK_RAN rather than CONTINUE: the condition guarded here is "the constraint already exists", which is a permanent
    and correct end state rather than a transient one worth retrying, so CONTINUE would log a warning on every startup
    for good.

    UPPER() on the name because PostgreSQL folds an unquoted identifier to lower case while MySQL keeps it as written,
    so the same constraint is stored under two spellings.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20260916083344-03-exercise-exactly-one-owner" author="krusche" dbms="mysql,postgresql">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <sqlCheck expectedResult="0">
                SELECT COUNT(*) FROM information_schema.table_constraints
                WHERE table_schema = ${lbSchema} AND table_name = 'exercise'
                  AND UPPER(constraint_name) = 'CHECK_EXERCISE_COURSE_OR_EXERCISE_GROUP'
            </sqlCheck>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>Enforce that an exercise belongs to a course or to an exercise group, never both and never neither.</comment>
        <sql>
            ALTER TABLE exercise ADD CONSTRAINT CHECK_EXERCISE_COURSE_OR_EXERCISE_GROUP
            CHECK ((course_id IS NOT NULL AND exercise_group_id IS NULL)
                OR (course_id IS NULL AND exercise_group_id IS NOT NULL));
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>ALTER TABLE exercise DROP CONSTRAINT CHECK_EXERCISE_COURSE_OR_EXERCISE_GROUP;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>


    <!--
    H2 is the no-Docker way of running the test suite (gradle/test.gradle, -Dzonky.test.database.type=H2), so it has
    to carry the constraint too: without it a run there passes on fixtures the other two engines reject, which is the
    opposite of what a second engine is for. It gets its own changeset rather than joining the dbms list above, because
    that precondition reads information_schema through a current-schema function that differs per engine and H2 folds
    an unquoted table name to upper case on top of it. There is nothing here for a precondition to guard: an H2
    database only ever exists for the length of a test run and is created from scratch.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20260916083344-04-exercise-exactly-one-owner-h2" author="krusche" dbms="h2">
        <comment>The same constraint on the in-process database the test suite can be pointed at.</comment>
        <sql>
            ALTER TABLE exercise ADD CONSTRAINT CHECK_EXERCISE_COURSE_OR_EXERCISE_GROUP
            CHECK ((course_id IS NOT NULL AND exercise_group_id IS NULL)
                OR (course_id IS NULL AND exercise_group_id IS NOT NULL));
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>ALTER TABLE exercise DROP CONSTRAINT CHECK_EXERCISE_COURSE_OR_EXERCISE_GROUP;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
